:::
:::


The degrees of freedom of each coefficient follow the level at which its predictor varies. Concern and the controls of the respondent vary between the respondents of every country-round, so the information about their slopes comes from tens of thousands of comparisons, and their degrees of freedom are of the order of the number of respondents. The variables of the country-round vary only between the 57 country-rounds, and their degrees of freedom are of that order. Whether a country is a long-term country of immigration varies only between the 16 countries, eleven of them coded 1 and five coded 0, and its test rests on 14.6 degrees of freedom: a coefficient of 0.54 with a standard error of 0.32 here (t = 1.68, p = 0.11), against 0.63 with a standard error of 0.19 and p = 0.008 in the paper. Sixteen countries, five of them on one side of the comparison, are all the evidence that a contextual difference of this kind can draw on, however many respondents each country holds, and in the refit that evidence does not reach the 5% level. A multilevel model counts the evidence for each predictor at its own level, which the regression on respondents without random intercepts does not. The [statistics module](stats-methods.qmd#sec-multilevel) shows the same pattern on the respondents of the EU-frames case, where the predictor of the claim varies only between country-years and the degrees of freedom of its slope fall from hundreds of thousands to a few hundred once that level is declared.

Task 3 of the case reproduces a model of this family, the model of the analyst 6U3LL, which drops the unemployment rate, on the file that Multi100 gave the analysts. The [statistics module](stats-methods.qmd#sec-trust) sets the random intercepts of this model beside the fixed effects of the curve and examines what sixteen countries do to the standard error of the coefficient of concern.

## 5. Composition and context {#sec-trust-context}

Across the countries, mean concern and mean distrust of parliament go together closely: over the 60 country-rounds the two means correlate at 0.69 (`display_country_means.csv`, design-weighted means). That association could be compositional, the slope of concern among individuals summed over countries whose citizens are more or less concerned, or it could hold something more, an association of the climate of a country with the distrust of everyone in it, whatever their own concern. The within–between model of the [statistics module](stats-methods.qmd#sec-re) separates the two. It enters concern twice, as the mean of each country and as the departure of each respondent from that mean, each with its own slope, beside the controls of the paper, the dummies for the rounds and random intercepts for countries and country-rounds:

```r
ref_frame <- ref_frame |> datawizard::demean(select = "x", by = "cntry")
within_between <- lmerTest::lmer(y ~ x_within + x_between + age + female + education + hh_income_z +
                                   unhappy + life_dissat + meet_rarely + soc_distrust + left_right +
                                   econ_dissat + income_difficulty + health_dissat + educ_dissat +
                                   winner + far_right_vote + factor(essround) + (1 | cntry) + (1 | cr),
                                 data = ref_frame)
```

The slope within countries is 0.175 points of distrust per point of concern (t = 41.2), and the slope between them −0.011 (t = −0.08 on 14.2 degrees of freedom), so that once the controls of the paper are held constant the country means of concern carry almost no association with distrust. The difference between the two slopes is the **contextual effect**, the association of the mean concern of a country with distrust beyond what the concern of each respondent accounts for. Fitted directly, by entering concern as answered beside its country mean, it is −0.187 (t = −1.31, p = 0.21).

Neither reading is rejected. A contextual effect of zero, under which the differences between countries are what the concern of their citizens leads one to expect, lies well inside the uncertainty of the estimate, and so does a slope between countries of zero, under which the country differences in concern go with no difference in distrust at all. The standard error of the slope between countries, 0.14, is close to the size of the slope within them, because sixteen countries supply sixteen observations of the country mean, however many respondents each holds. The close correlation of the raw country means is therefore no evidence of a contextual effect: it disappears once the controls are held constant, and the data cannot say whether what remains is composition or nothing. Testing a contextual proposition on these data needs either more countries or a design that varies the context within them, as the panel of the EU-frames case varies unemployment within each country from year to year.

## 6. The simulated twin of the EU-frames case {#sec-twin}

The EU-frames case takes its claim from a study by the original author (OA) of that case, and from this section on OA refers to that author [@Teney2016DoesEUEconomic]. Its central result comes from a model fitted to individual survey respondents, and the Eurobarometer microdata behind it are licensed by GESIS and cannot be redistributed. They can still be obtained: the sixteen survey files can be downloaded with a free GESIS account, and the build script in the workspace turns them into one respondent file, `data_private/eb_respondents.rds`, whose [codebook](../cases/eu-frames/data/eb_respondents_codebook.md) is published even though the file is not. The [data sources module](data-sources.qmd#sec-gesis) explains the download, and the [data management module](data-management.qmd) the build. Every number on this page labelled 'real' was computed on that file by a script in the course repository, and a reader who holds the file can rerun the script.

What the course ships is a **synthetic twin**: `EUframes_person_full.csv`, 41,660 simulated respondents across the real 270 country-years, with every variable that the published regressions of OA use. No real respondent appears in it. [The simulation module](simulation.qmd#sec-dgp) explains how it is built. In short, a respondent's characteristics come from a correlation structure estimated on the real file, and their answers from a model of the survey instrument conditional on those characteristics. The code chunks of sections 7 to 12 run on the twin, so that the page renders for everyone, and swapping the twin for the respondent file is a one-line change.

A number from the twin is licensed by its validation against the real respondents: the same model was fitted to the real respondents and to the twin, and the two sets of coefficients were compared.


::: {#tbl-validation .cell tbl-cap='Every coefficient of OA\'s published model, fitted on 416,698 real respondents and on the twin. The last column is the largest difference over the two-sample standard error.'}

```{.r .cell-code}
valid |>
  dplyr::summarise(
    `coefficients compared` = dplyr::n(),
    `within 1 standard error` = sum(abs(z) < 1),
    `within 2 standard errors` = sum(abs(z) < 2),
    `largest discrepancy` = round(max(abs(z)), 2))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
# A tibble: 1 × 4
  `coefficients compared` `within 1 standard error` `within 2 standard errors`
                    <int>                     <int>                      <int>
1                      64                        46                         60
# ℹ 1 more variable: `largest discrepancy` <dbl>
```


:::
:::


60 of 64 agree within two standard errors, where roughly three would be expected to fall outside by chance alone. The comparison is stored in `_model-outputs/twin_validation.csv` and can be read line by line.

Two cautions apply to that result. The yardstick above is the **two-sample** standard error, which asks whether a difference exceeds the sampling noise of the twin itself, the question that a reader quoting one of its numbers has. Judged instead against the precision that 416,698 respondents afford, only 39 agree, because the twin holds a tenth as many people and cannot stand in at full precision. And the model validated is OA's specification with its cross-level interactions removed. The twin reproduces that model, but it is not validated for the interacted form and should not be quoted for it.

